      "reason": "My only evaluated change missed the target. It rewrote RegexTokenizer._encode_ordinary to encode the uncached chunks as a set and then join the cached ids with chain/map in C. Output stayed byte-identical, but encode_ir got worse (ratio 1.0070) and train_ir did too (1.0017). The budget is spent, so I am stopping. Most of the instruction count looks like fixed interpreter and import cost, and earlier generations have already optimised the hot paths. Next things to try: give the RegexTokenizer.train per-chunk loop a fast path for chunks where the pair occurs once (skip the merge() call), and make tie-breaking in BasicTokenizer.train find the first candidate pair in C, e.g. next(filter(cands.__contains__, zip(ids, ids[1:])))."
